      "id": 12,
      "options": {
        "content": "## Reading this dashboard\n\n**Pick a run.** Use the `run (testid)` selector at the top. Every run is tagged\n`testid=<utc timestamp>-<type>`, and the runner prints a ready-made link that opens\nthis dashboard already zoomed to that run's exact window and filtered to its testid.\nUse it: it is the difference between \"Grafana is broken\" and \"I was looking at the\nlast 30 minutes\".\n\n**Why a panel can look empty.** k6 only writes samples *while the test is running*.\nIt pushes every 2s and once more at the end, and then the series simply stops. So:\n\n| You see | Because |\n|---|---|\n| Nothing, before you start | There is no run yet - that is correct |\n| Nothing, minutes after a run | The time range no longer covers the run. Widen it, or use the runner's link |\n| Nothing, while a run is in progress | The first push takes ~2s; also check the k6 log for remote-write errors |\n| Only the last run | The `testid` selector is set to one value - set it to `All` to overlay runs |\n\n**Latency percentiles: two modes.** These panels prefer\n`histogram_quantile(...rate(k6_http_req_duration_seconds...))`, i.e. Prometheus\n**native histograms**, which give true per-interval percentiles. If native\nhistograms are off, they fall back (`or`) to k6's gauge statistics\n(`k6_http_req_duration_p95`) - and those are **cumulative over the run**: they\nconverge within seconds and then stop moving, so they cannot show a spike or a\nslow drift. A flat latency line in gauge mode is not evidence that latency was\nflat.\n\nTo enable native histograms:\n\n- `k6/docker-compose.yml` - `K6_PROMETHEUS_RW_TREND_AS_NATIVE_HISTOGRAM=true`\n- Prometheus needs `--enable-feature=native-histograms` (already set in the compose command)\n\n## When there is no data at all\n\nWork down this list - it isolates the broken layer.\n\n1. **Did k6 send anything?** <http://127.0.0.1:9090/api/v1/label/__name__/values> - search for `k6_`.\n   Nothing there means the problem is the k6 output, not Grafana.\n2. **Did the run report a push error?** `results/<run>/k6.log` - k6 writes remote-write\n   failures as `level=error`. A missing `--enable-feature=native-histograms` shows up here.\n3. **Is the datasource right?** <http://127.0.0.1:3000/connections/datasources> - there must be\n   exactly one `Prometheus` pointing at `http://prometheus:9090`. A second, hand-made\n   datasource with no URL renders every panel empty.\n4. **Use `127.0.0.1`, not `localhost`.** Docker Desktop publishes on IPv6 as well, and\n   its IPv6 proxy does not answer here, so `http://localhost:3000` hangs while\n   `http://127.0.0.1:3000` works.\n\n## What this dashboard is not\n\nIt shows the **generator's** view. It cannot tell you *why* the target was slow.\nFor that you need the target's own CPU, memory, GC and pool metrics on the same\ntimeline - see the \"Observability for the system under test\" item in the main README.",
        "mode": "markdown"
      },
      "title": "How to use this dashboard",
      "type": "text"
    }
